#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# hai-meta — the "harness on top of the harness" controller.
#
# This repository IS the HAI-Harness product (the templates under Agents/ and
# Human/, plus the bin/hai-harness.mjs installer, are what ship to other
# projects). To develop the product itself we dogfood it: we install a *second*,
# local harness instance into .hai/ and drive development from there.
#
#   - The INNER harness  = the product, i.e. this repo's tracked files.
#   - The OUTER harness   = the .hai/ instance you work in to build the product.
#
# Self-improving loop: when you improve the product's scaffold (role docs,
# onboarding, skills) using the outer harness, run `./hai-meta sync` and the
# outer harness extracts those updates and refreshes itself — without touching
# your in-flight working files (brief, planning, tasks, handoffs, lessons).
#
# Project context (.hai/, AGENTS.override.md, CLAUDE.md) and this helper are
# version-controlled for transparency. Machine-local settings and receipts stay
# ignored; development context is excluded from the installable package.
#
# Usage:
#   ./hai-meta bootstrap [--force]   Create or repair the .hai/ meta-harness.
#   ./hai-meta sync       [--force]   Pull the latest product scaffold into .hai/.
#   ./hai-meta doctor                 Check that .hai/ has the expected files.
#   ./hai-meta help

set -euo pipefail

ROOT="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)"
HAI="$ROOT/.hai"
CLI="$ROOT/bin/hai-harness.mjs"
FORCE=0

# --- seed writers -----------------------------------------------------------
# Each writes the meta-specific content for one file. Delimiters are single
# quoted so backticks and $ inside the markdown are emitted literally.

write_project_context() {
  cat > "$1" <<'PCONTEXT'
# Project Context

<!--
This is the OUTER harness (.hai/). The project it is building is the
HAI-Harness product in the parent directory. Keep this file durable and
architectural. It is not a sprint log.
-->

Shared high-level context for every agent working in the `.hai/` meta-harness.

## 1. What this project is

- **Product under development:** HAI-Harness — a repo-as-truth collaboration
  layer for humans and AI agents.
- **Where the product lives:** the parent repository, one level up from `.hai/`.
  `.hai/` is a version-controlled *instance* of the harness that we use to build the
  product. We are using the harness to build the harness.
- **Who it is for:** teams (human + AI) who want an independent harness layer
  governing both, instead of relying on model memory or chat history.

## 2. The two layers (read this carefully)

- **Inner harness = the product = the parent repo's tracked files.** This is
  what workers edit and what gets committed:
  - `../AGENTS.md` — the root pointer any AI reads first in an installed project.
  - `../Agents/` — the agent operating-layer **template** that ships to users
    (onboarding, role docs `claudia.md`/`augustus.md`/`julius.md`, `skills/`,
    and the `tasks/`, `handoffs/`, `lessons/` scaffolding).
  - `../Human/` — the human-workspace **template** that ships to users.
  - `../bin/hai-harness.mjs` — the installer CLI (`init`, `update`, `doctor`).
  - `../README.md`, `../package.json`, `../LICENSE`.
- **Outer harness = this `.hai/` instance** — the operating layer for *this*
  development effort. Its `planning.md`, `tasks/`, `handoffs/`, `lessons/`, and
  the `Human/` brief here describe how we build the product. This context is
  version-controlled but excluded from the installable package.
- **Session routing:** the root tracked `AGENTS.override.md` directs Codex
  here. Start with `.hai/AGENTS.md`; never use the tracked root `AGENTS.md` or
  tracked `Agents/` tree as this repository's live operating instructions.

## 3. Hard boundaries

- `../Agents/` and `../Human/` are **product source templates**, not this
  effort's working space. Never put this effort's briefs, plans, or task notes
  into them. Plan and track *here*, in `.hai/`.
- Workers (Augustus, Julius) make product changes by editing the parent files
  listed above. Claudia plans here and never edits product source.
- **Git boundary:** track the product source, `.hai/` project context, root
  redirects, and `hai-meta`. Ignore machine-local settings and receipts. Keep
  development context out of the installable package and inner templates.

## 4. The self-improving loop

When a worker improves the product scaffold (role docs, onboarding, `skills/`),
this outer harness can pull those improvements into itself:

```sh
cd ..        # repo root
./hai-meta sync
```

`sync` runs the product's own `hai-harness update` against `.hai/` (refreshing
`AGENTS.md`, `Agents/onboarding.md`, the role docs, and `Human/onboarding.md`)
and then mirrors `Agents/skills/`. It never overwrites this effort's working
files (`project_context.md`, `planning.md`, `Human/brief.md`, `tasks/`,
`handoffs/`, `lessons/`). To recreate or repair the instance, run
`./hai-meta bootstrap` from the repo root.

## 5. Core project rules

- Always read a file before editing it.
- Never merge or push to `main` unless the user explicitly asks.
- Cheap verification for product changes: `node --check ../bin/hai-harness.mjs`
  and `node ../bin/hai-harness.mjs help`. Treat anything networked or
  long-running as high-cost (opt-in only).
- The repo is the only source of truth. Do not act on chat history or assumed
  memory — write decisions down here.

## 6. Collaboration map

- [onboarding.md](onboarding.md) is the mandatory first read for every agent.
- [planning.md](planning.md) is Claudia-owned and the single iteration source of
  truth.
- `tasks/` holds current worker assignments; `handoffs/` holds baton passes;
  `lessons/` holds reusable learnings — all scoped to building the product.
- `../Human/` (the product template) is not agent context. The human workspace
  for *this* effort is `Human/` inside `.hai/`.
PCONTEXT
}

write_brief() {
  cat > "$1" <<'BRIEF'
# Product Brief — HAI-Harness

<!--
The durable product thesis for the thing we are building (the HAI-Harness
product in the parent repo). This is the human workspace for the meta effort.
-->

## 1. What it is

- **Product name:** HAI-Harness.
- **One-sentence description:** a collaboration architecture (a repository
  overlay) that gives humans and AI agents one shared, repo-as-truth operating
  system.
- **What it helps users do:** run reliable human+AI collaboration without
  depending on model memory or chat history — plan, execute, hand off, and
  capture lessons through files on disk.
- **What it is not:** a runtime dependency or a framework that replaces your app
  structure. It is an overlay (`Agents/` + `Human/` + a root `AGENTS.md`
  pointer) installed alongside existing project files.
- **Who it is for:** product teams where humans and AI are peers and both need
  accurate, durable context.

## 2. Core insight

- Humans and AI don't need *more* context — they need *accurate* context. The
  repository is the absolute source of truth: if a decision isn't written in the
  repo, it doesn't exist.
- Current tools lean on model memory and long chat windows. Models suffer
  "amnesiac intern syndrome" as context bloats; humans forget why decisions were
  made. Both drift from the original plan.
- The fix is structure: stable role docs, disposable chat sessions, explicit
  handoffs, and active skills that maintain the system instead of passive docs
  everyone ignores.

## 3. Meta-goal (why this `.hai/` instance exists)

We build the harness *with* the harness. Every improvement we make to the
product should be one we first felt the need for while dogfooding it here. After
shipping a scaffold improvement, run `../hai-meta sync` so this instance adopts
it — closing the loop.

## 4. Roadmap targets (from README; Claudia to triage in planning.md)

- **Layer 2 — Concurrency & Team Mode:** isolated worker workspaces,
  point-to-point messaging, strict state-machine routing so workers can't touch
  code until the planner approves the dependency graph.
- **Layer 3 — The Evaluator:** a dedicated adversarial agent in an isolated
  sandbox whose only job is to try to break a worker's output.
- **Agentic infra:** background auto-sweeping to dedupe/compress `lessons/`, and
  pluggable CI-style hooks that halt an agent when it breaks an architectural
  rule.
BRIEF
}

